# XSH pthread_once()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Every once_control has static storage duration and is initialized with
# PTHREAD_ONCE_INIT (anything else is undefined).

assertion  XSH.pthread_once.001
anchor     functions/pthread_once.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_once.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For each once_control, init_routine is called by the first
           pthread_once() only: later calls with it, from the same thread
           or another, do not call it again; a second once_control has
           its own first call. Every call returns 0.
test       t/001-once.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_once.002
anchor     functions/pthread_once.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Four threads calling pthread_once() together with one
           once_control each return only after init_routine (300 ms long)
           has completed, and it ran once.
test       t/002-completes.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_once.003
anchor     functions/pthread_once.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       When init_routine is canceled at a cancellation point (blocked in
           read()), the once_control is as if pthread_once() had never been
           called: the next pthread_once() with it calls its init_routine
           and returns 0.
test       t/003-cancel-init.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_once.004
anchor     functions/pthread_once.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       A thread waiting in pthread_once() while another runs
           init_routine, interrupted by a caught signal (SIGUSR1, without
           SA_RESTART), does not get EINTR: it returns 0 after
           init_routine has completed.
note       The signal is sent 150 ms after the waiter is about to call
           pthread_once(), while init_routine still has 250 ms to run.
test       t/004-no-eintr.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_once.005
anchor     functions/pthread_once.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
says       pthread_once() is not a cancellation point: a thread with a
           deferred cancellation request pending calls it (init_routine
           runs no cancellation point), returns from it, and is canceled
           only at its next pthread_testcancel().
test       t/005-not-cancel-point.c
